From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mzak <mzak.amin@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 04:04:15 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 11/12] hwmon: qcom-bcl-hwmon: probe only with enable=1
 (default off)

The PMIC BCL monitor programs alarm thresholds and takes interrupts on
the PMIH0108 BCL block. On the OnePlus Pad 4 it is new and untested, so
make it opt-in: without qcom_bcl_hwmon.enable=1 (command line or a
modprobe option) the probe logs one line and returns -ENODEV before it
touches the hardware. The device tree node stays, so turning it on needs
no other change.

Signed-off-by: mzak <mzak.amin@gmail.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/hwmon/qcom-bcl-hwmon.c | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/hwmon/qcom-bcl-hwmon.c b/drivers/hwmon/qcom-bcl-hwmon.c
index 33bbdd8196d1..03fed448e034 100644
--- a/drivers/hwmon/qcom-bcl-hwmon.c
+++ b/drivers/hwmon/qcom-bcl-hwmon.c
@@ -1328,12 +1328,27 @@ static int bcl_regmap_field_init(struct device *dev, struct bcl_device *bcl,
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Off by default on this kernel (iceland): the BCL block is only read and
+ * its alarm thresholds programmed with qcom_bcl_hwmon.enable=1 (command line
+ * or modprobe option), so the device tree node changes nothing until the
+ * driver has been tested on the OnePlus Pad 4.
+ */
+static bool enable;
+module_param(enable, bool, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(enable, "Probe the PMIC BCL monitor (default: off)");
+
 static int bcl_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	struct bcl_device *bcl;
 	int ret;
 	u32 reg;
 
+	if (!enable) {
+		dev_info(&pdev->dev, "off (qcom_bcl_hwmon.enable=1 switches it on)\n");
+		return -ENODEV;
+	}
+
 	bcl = devm_kzalloc(&pdev->dev, sizeof(*bcl), GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!bcl)
 		return -ENOMEM;
